<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 28px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 16px; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International Communication</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Communicating Panama's Importance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain complex ideas with simple examples. Concept: Clarify abstract concepts, using practical illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain Panama's global importance using simple visuals like maps and charts (e.g., uses a map to show how the Panama Canal reduces shipping routes between Asia and Europe by thousands of miles). Can clarify abstract concepts on international trade with practical illustrations (e.g., illustrates "trade routes" by drawing arrows on a map). Can use body language and bilingual keywords to aid in comprehension (e.g., gestures to indicate trade flow and explains terms in both English and Spanish).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map and points to Panama. Students are asked to share what they already know about Panama's global importance in international trade, using keywords like "canal," "hub," or "transit." The teacher encourages them to use simple English phrases. For example, "Panama is a key area because of the canal."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a complex idea simply using a visual. For example, pointing to the Panama Canal on a map, the teacher says: "The Panama Canal is recognized for its critical role in shipping. It reduces global shipping routes significantly, impacting international commerce." The teacher draws an arrow showing a shortcut to illustrate. Pronunciation of /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global" is emphasized. The teacher then models how to clarify by saying, "To clarify, 'shipping routes' means the paths ships take across the ocean."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea of the Panama Canal's importance?" (Expected: It saves time/distance for ships.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'trade hub,' what does that mean for companies?" (Expected: It's a central place where many companies do business, connecting different areas.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short excerpt from a business presentation about Panama's potential for attracting multinational companies, specifically focusing on financial services and logistics. The text uses passive voice to introduce Panama's role, modals of necessity for business requirements, and relative clauses to add information. (e.g., "Panama is considered a prime location for financial services due to its strategic position, which attracts many international companies. Professionals must be proficient in English to engage with these multinational firms.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Lead presentations on Panama's global role, ensuring all viewpoints are acknowledged and effectively communicated.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the excerpt and answer gist questions to identify abstract concepts or complex ideas:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the two main sectors mentioned that make Panama important for international companies?" (Expected: Financial services and logistics.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What skill is emphasized as necessary for professionals in Panama?" (Expected: Proficiency in English.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What makes Panama a trade hub that connects continents?" (Expected: Its strategic position and infrastructure like the canal.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using passive voice ("Panama is recognized..."), modals of necessity ("must be proficient..."), and relative clauses ("a trade hub that connects...") to explain and clarify concepts related to Panama's global importance and business relations. Target vocabulary includes: <i>relevance, biodiversity, financial services, multinational, impact, strategic, significant, require, engage.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students complete sentences that explain concepts using the target grammar. For example: "Panama ____ (recognize) for its diverse biodiversity, ____ (which) attracts many ecotourists. To work in international companies, professionals ____ (must) be proficient in English." The teacher checks for accurate passive voice and modal usage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Concept Clarification (Semi-controlled):</b> In pairs, students receive cards with complex terms from the project's context (e.g., "geostrategic impact," "sustainable commerce," "financial hub"). Partner A explains the term using simpler words, passive voice, and relative clauses, while Partner B provides a practical illustration or example. They must use at least one specific vocabulary word from the list. (e.g., "A 'financial hub' is an area where many financial services are concentrated, which allows companies to engage in international commerce easily.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Visual Explanation (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students are given simple maps or charts (e.g., showing trade routes, economic indicators). In pairs, they take turns explaining the information presented on the visual to their partner, as if their partner doesn't understand. They must use gestures and body language to aid comprehension, and can use one bilingual keyword if absolutely necessary (e.g., "This chart shows how multinational companies require a stable *sector* - *sector* means 'area' of business - to invest."). Partner B checks for clarity and offers suggestions for simplification. Pronunciation of stress in Wh- question words (e.g., "WHAT MAKES Panama...") is encouraged.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Multinational Business Proposal Explanation." Students work in small groups (3-4) on their "Multinational Business Proposal" from the 21st Century Skills Project. Each group acts as a Panamanian delegation presenting their proposal to a panel of "international investors" (other groups or teacher). The focus is not on presenting the entire proposal, but on one group member mediating a specific section of the proposal (e.g., "The Financial Benefits," "Logistics and Trade Routes," or "Environmental Impact") for a diverse audience. They must simplify complex ideas, clarify abstract concepts, and use visuals (e.g., a hand-drawn chart, a simple map) to aid understanding. They are encouraged to use body language and can use a very limited number of bilingual keywords if a concept is truly difficult to convey. They must use passive voice, modals of necessity, and relative clauses from the target grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example prompt:</b> "Explain how Panama's biodiversity is a valuable asset for sustainable commerce in our proposal. Make sure the investors, who might not be scientists, fully understand."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students lead the explanations, ensuring concepts like "sustainable development" or "global supply chain" are effectively communicated. Stress modal verbs like "MUST" when emphasizing key requirements in the proposal (e.g., "Companies MUST respect local customs to ensure successful operations here").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student, within their group, selects one abstract concept or complex idea from their "Multinational Business Proposal" (e.g., "economic impact of transit sector," "challenges of international shipping," "relevance of financial services"). They prepare a brief (1-2 minute) mediated explanation of this concept for a hypothetical audience who is unfamiliar with business jargon, using a simple visual aid (e.g., a quick drawing, a few bullet points on a mini-whiteboard or paper). Their peers will observe and provide feedback. The teacher will also use an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Assessment):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Were complex ideas explained with simple examples? (Specific Objective: Concept explanation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of practical illustrations/visuals (Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar: Passive voice, modals of necessity, relative clauses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary: (e.g., <i>trade, relevance, hub, financial services, impact, strategic</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Intelligible pronunciation of /t/ and /g/, appropriate stress on Wh- questions and modal verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of body language and appropriate tone to aid comprehension (Learning Outcome).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Language shows respect for different opinions or knowledge levels (Pragmatic Competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One concept I successfully simplified today was _____, and I did this by _____."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time I mediate information, I will focus on _____ to improve my clarity."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using visuals and simple examples help you explain complex ideas about Panama's global role today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when trying to clarify abstract concepts, and how did you try to overcome them?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What strategies for mediation worked best (e.g., using maps, drawing arrows, using simple analogies)? What specific abstract concepts or complex ideas were most challenging for students to mediate? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks to better support students in simplifying professional or academic content, perhaps by providing more varied visual aids or structured sentence starters for clarification phrases? How can the "Multinational Business Proposal" be further developed or presented to a wider audience?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Communicating Panama's Importance" theme, consolidating all skills into a mediation task. The next unit will likely focus on a new theme, but the mediation skills practiced here, especially explaining complex information, will be crucial for any future academic or professional communication tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>